<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of Canudos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1896">1896</a>–<a href="/years/1897">1897</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of Canudos was a conflict between the Brazilian Republic and the religious community of Canudos in the backlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a>, founded by the preacher Antônio Conselheiro. The settlement, which grew to many thousands of poor followers, defeated three military expeditions in 1896–1897 before a fourth, much larger force destroyed it in October 1897, killing most of its inhabitants. Euclides da Cunha described the war in Os Sertões.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-canudos.jpg" alt="War of Canudos" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Rsabbatini at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canudos-map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Canudos, which its inhabitants called Belo Monte, lay in the semi-arid sertão of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a>, a poor region of subsistence farming and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> raising that suffered severe droughts. For the sertanejos the republic proclaimed on 15 November 1889 brought chiefly higher taxes, and the deposed emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil">Pedro II</a> was still loved by the common people. Antônio Vicente Mendes Maciel, known as Antônio Conselheiro, was one of many itinerant preachers of the backcountry. He held that monarchs ruled by God-given right and predicted the return of the Portuguese king Sebastian, and local landowners and the Church hierarchy saw him as a threat.</p><p>In 1893 he settled with his followers at Canudos on the Vaza-Barris River, a site that was hard to reach. Within two years it had become the second-largest urban centre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a>, living from crops and the export of leather. The press branded its people monarchists who planned to restore the monarchy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The conflict began over lumber ordered from Juazeiro for a new church. The judge Arlindo Leoni blocked the delivery and asked Governor Luis Viana for police, claiming the town faced an invasion. In November 1896 a force of about 100 men under Pires Ferreira was surprised at Uauá by between 1,000 and 3,000 followers armed with old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a>, pikes and scythes, and withdrew to Juazeiro. President Prudente de Morais then sent federal troops. A force that left Juazeiro on 12 January 1897 was surrounded and fell back to Monte Santo, while the settlement grew to over 30,000 residents.</p><p>Colonel Antônio Moreira César followed with 1,300 men, but they lost their way among the huts, he was mortally wounded, and the retreat of 6 March 1897 became a rout. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> rioters destroyed four monarchist newspaper offices. General Arthur Oscar de Andrade Guimarães then led the final campaign, in which more than 8,000 soldiers besieged and starved the settlement until the fighting ended on 5 October 1897.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Antônio Conselheiro had probably died of dysentery on 22 September. His body was exhumed and his head displayed on a pike. Men who surrendered on the promise of their lives were hacked to death, the soldiers burned the settlement, and about 150 survivors remained. Estimates of the dead range from about 30,000, given by Euclides da Cunha, to some 15,000 according to Robert M. Levine and 5,000 according to Joel Singer.</p><p>Walnice Nogueira Galvão sees the war's main result as the consolidation of the republic, though public opinion later recognised that the peasants had no tie to real monarchists. The town's site now lies under the reservoir of the Cocorobó Dam, built in the 1960s, and the Canudos State Park, established in 1986, preserves many places of the war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War of Canudos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
